is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The `iptables` FORWARD chain on `router6`, specifically applied to traffic arriving on interface `eth2` (the `r6_r3` uplink connecting to `router3` and the rest of the WAN).

**Mechanism:** Router6 acts as the gateway for the `172.18.51.0/24` subnet (directly attached on `eth1` via `mswitch2`). An ACL on its FORWARD chain contains four rules scoped to `iifname eth2`: rule 1 accepts TCP port 80 from `172.16.10.0/24` to `172.18.51.1` only; rules 2–4 then drop **all TCP, all UDP, and all other traffic (including ICMP)** arriving from the upstream side. Because every packet from any external host — pc4, laptop0, pc11–18 in other subnets, and every intermediate router — must transit `eth2` to be forwarded into the `172.18.51.x` network, all of them are silently dropped. Router6 can itself reach `172.18.51.17` because its own-originated pings use the OUTPUT hook, bypassing the FORWARD chain entirely. The drop counters (35 ICMP packets × 84 bytes = 2940 bytes; 75 UDP packets = 4500 bytes) directly corroborate traffic being shed during this diagnostic.

**Localization:** `router6`, FORWARD chain, interface `eth2`.

**Root cause:** A restrictive iptables ACL on `router6`'s FORWARD chain blocks all traffic forwarded in from the WAN-side interface (`eth2`), rendering the entire `172.18.51.0/24` subnet unreachable from all external hosts and routers.

**Fix:** Remove rules 2–4 from the FORWARD chain on `router6` (`iptables -D FORWARD` for the three DROP rules on `iifname eth2`), retaining only the specific port-80 allow if required by policy, so that general traffic can be forwarded into the `172.18.51.0/24` segment.
=== END ===